window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][98] = "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Accept the management reversal and launch</td>\n<td>Schedule and the customer relationship</td>\n<td>The risk the engineers described</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Hold the recommendation not to launch with the O-rings below 53 degrees, with the evidence in one clear picture, and require the decision-makers to decide with full information</td>\n<td>Safety</td>\n<td>Delay; friction with the customer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Escalate: ensure that the senior officials hear the engineers' position and the reversal directly before the decision</td>\n<td>Informed decision</td>\n<td>Time; organizational tension</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B and C cost time and relationships and protect against an irreversible loss. A saves the schedule at the risk of a catastrophic tail.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the launch was recommended by Thiokol management and approved, and the launch took place on 28 January.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: the loss of the vehicle and crew; the Commission's conclusion on the technical cause (the failure of the pressure seal in the aft field joint of the right Solid Rocket Motor) and its findings on the decision-making process, including that Thiokol's management reversed its position at the urging of Marshall and contrary to the views of its engineers <code>[V: Rogers Commission, Volume 1]</code>; the Commission's recommendations to NASA were not read for this case.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the engineers' dissent reached the final decision-makers, the Commission concluded that, had the decision-makers known all of the facts, it was highly unlikely they would have decided to launch <code>[V: Rogers Commission, Chapter V]</code>. A reader should be careful with hindsight: the participants did not know the outcome, and the case is complicated by technical uncertainty. The method to carry away for your own work: put the conclusion first and the evidence in one clear picture; frame decisions so that the burden falls on showing safety when the outcome is irreversible; make incentives visible; and make sure that dissent reaches the person who decides, in writing.</p>\n<span id=\"c32-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Eleven questions. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), and every person, company and figure is invented (invented) to show structure. Each ends with follow-ups and answers. Where a real company's practice is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-01 · Beginner · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Summarize a complex product decision for a non-technical executive in sixty seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Starts with the history and technical background; the decision comes late.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the decision and the main reason first, with a number.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Answer first, two or three named supports in plain language, the risk and the ask, and a closing restatement, within the time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;We decided to add a second payment provider, and I need your approval to start next week. Three reasons. First, reliability: our single provider failed for four hours last quarter and we lost about $180,000 in sales. Second, cost: a second provider lets us negotiate, and we estimate a 10 to 15% fall in processing fees, about $300,000 a year. Third, speed: the work is six engineer-weeks, which fits this quarter. The main risk is a more complex checkout, which we'd mitigate with a staged rollout to 10% of customers first. So: add the second provider, start next week, rollout in stages. Can I have your approval today?&quot; (about 100 words; about 45 seconds).</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Answer and ask first; three named reasons in plain terms, each with a number; the risk with a mitigation; the ask repeated.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if she asks, &quot;Why not wait a quarter?&quot; <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Because the next outage costs about $45,000 an hour at peak, and each quarter without a second provider is a risk of that. I can wait if you prefer, and I'd want us to agree the date and the reversal condition: if another outage happens, we start immediately.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Which number would you lead with if you only had ten seconds? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The $180,000 we lost in one outage: it makes the problem concrete, and the rest follows from it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-02 · Beginner · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you structure a weekly update to your manager?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I list what I did this week.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Status, what changed, what's next and what's needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Headline with status and the ask, news with impact (not activity), an explicit &quot;need from you&quot; with a deadline, brevity, and a link for the detail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Four lines, headline first. The headline gives status and whether I need anything: 'On track for the 12th; one decision needed.' Then <em>what changed</em> since last week, with the impact: not 'met with legal' but 'legal flagged a consent wording issue; fix is a one-line copy change.' Then <em>what's next</em>, two items. Then <em>what I need from you</em>, with a deadline: 'Approve the copy change by Monday.' If I need nothing, I say so, since 'nothing needed' is useful news. I keep it under 80 words and link a running log for detail, so my manager can stop reading after the headline if all is well.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Reader's time respected; news not activity; an explicit ask; honesty about status.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if there is nothing new? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I say so in one line: 'On track, no changes, nothing needed.' A short update is a good sign, and I keep the habit of writing so that they can trust the silence.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the news is bad? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The headline says it: 'At risk of slipping two days.' I do not save it for the end. I tell my manager by message or in person the day I know, and the weekly update repeats it with the plan.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-03 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you get an engineer to build something they don't want to build?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I explain why it's important until they agree.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Understands the engineer's concern and finds a shared goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the ladder (incentives, shared goal, evidence, pre-wire, clear small ask, credit), trades scope openly, accepts a no that is right, and keeps the relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I start by finding out why they don't want to. It is usually a real reason: risk to the system, maintenance burden, a better use of their time, or distrust that the priority is real. So I ask, 'What worries you about this?' and listen. Then I look for the goal we share: if the feature is to cut failed payments, I show the numbers (3% of payments fail, costing about $40,000 a month) and ask how they'd solve it, since their way may be better than my feature. I offer to trade scope: 'If we cut the report export, does the rest fit in two weeks?' I make the ask small and concrete: 'Can you spend half a day to estimate it and tell me what would make it risky?' If their answer is that it's a bad idea, I take it seriously and ask what evidence would change their mind; sometimes I am wrong. If we still disagree, I say who decides, which is me for what, and they flag the risk in writing. And I give credit: when it ships, I say whose idea made it safer.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Listening; shared goal; evidence with limits; trade of scope; respect; credit.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if they are simply overloaded? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the question is priority, not persuasion. I show what we would drop to make room, ask the engineering lead to agree the tradeoff with me, and if the work doesn't make the cut, I accept it. Forcing it through burns trust.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do you do if you can't persuade them? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I check whether I understand their objection; if I do and still believe the feature matters, I take the tradeoff to our shared manager with both views stated fairly and ask for a decision. I tell the engineer I'm doing it and why.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-04 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE · Amazon</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You need senior leaders to approve a major change. How would you prepare the written proposal, and what goes first?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A slide deck with the main points.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A short document with the ask and recommendation first.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A narrative decision document with the ask, context, options, a recommendation with reasons, what would change the recommendation, risks and next steps; drafted early, reviewed by outsiders, cut, with numbers and a pre-wired reader list.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I describe a practice Amazon has publicly described, narrative memos in place of slides, and make no claim about any internal process <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon 2017 shareholder letter]</code>. &quot;I'd write a decision document, one to two pages, not slides. The first lines are the decision I need, from whom, by when, and my recommendation, so a reader who stops there knows what to do. Then the context in a handful of sentences with the one number that matters. Then the options, usually three, including doing nothing, each with cost, benefit and risk. Then why I recommend one, and what evidence would change my mind. Then risks and next steps with owners.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd allow real time: a good memo takes days, not hours, because writing in full sentences exposes the gaps in my thinking. I'd draft early, ask two people outside the project to read it and tell me the point, cut a third, and check every number against its source. Before the meeting I'd pre-wire the two or three people most likely to object and fold their points into the options. I'd bring the memo to the meeting to be read, not presented, and expect the discussion to start from it.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The ask first; options with costs; humility about what would change the view; real writing effort; pre-wiring.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How long would it take you? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;About a week for something this significant: two days to gather evidence and decide, two to draft, a day of review, a day to cut and check numbers. If I'm told the decision is tomorrow, I write the one-page version and say which parts are less certain.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you know it's good? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A reader outside the team can say the decision and the main reason after one read; the numbers have sources; each option has a real cost; and a skeptic in review couldn't find an option I'd left out. If they can, I rewrite.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-05 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A senior stakeholder insists on a feature you think will hurt users. You've said no twice. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd keep saying no,&quot; or &quot;I'd just build it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Seeks to understand the goal, proposes evidence and a test, and escalates if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Restates their goal, separates facts from values, agrees criteria in advance, runs the cheapest test, disagrees and commits if overruled, and escalates openly if the harm is serious.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Twice saying no means the conversation isn't working, so I'd change it. First, restate their goal: 'I think you want to raise sign-ups before the quarter ends. Is that right?' and ask what's behind the feature. Often there is a goal I can serve another way. Second, separate what data can settle (does it lower retention?) from what's a judgment (how much do we value short-term sign-ups?). Third, propose a cheap test with criteria agreed in advance: 'Let's try it for 10% of users for two weeks; if retention at day 14 falls by more than a point, we stop.' That turns a fight into an experiment and is hard to refuse.</p>\n<p>&quot;If they still want it without a test, I would ask who decides. If it is them, I'd say plainly what I think will happen, put my concern and the reversal condition in writing, and commit to doing it well, with monitoring. If I believed it would harm users seriously or break a policy or law, I would raise it with the next level or the relevant owner, openly and telling the stakeholder I am doing so, and I wouldn't build the part I thought was unlawful.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Understanding their goal; a test; commitment; escalation for real harm only.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the test is positive and you were wrong? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I was wrong and I say so, thank them for pushing, and ship it. The reason to test was to find out which of us was right, and I'd rather learn it in two weeks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> When is escalating the wrong move? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;When it is a disagreement about priorities or taste and the decision is theirs. Escalation is for decisions that carry safety, legal or large user harm, not for disagreements I lost.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-06 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Two senior stakeholders want opposite things from your product, and you can't satisfy both. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd go with the more senior one,&quot; or &quot;I'd find a compromise.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Understands each goal, looks for a shared goal and brings data.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Maps both, finds each one's real objective, offers options that serve both goals partly, agrees decision criteria, brings the choice to the common decision-maker with a recommendation, and keeps both informed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd begin by understanding each goal separately and in depth. Suppose the head of sales wants a longer free trial to raise conversion, and the head of finance wants a shorter one to protect cash. I would ask each what they're optimizing, what number they would watch, and what would change their mind. Usually the disagreement is over different time horizons or measures, not over facts.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then I'd look for a shared goal: 'We both want profitable growth.' I'd turn the question into options with numbers: 7 days, 14 days, or 14 days with a day-7 reminder; and agree with both the criteria beforehand, for example, 'payback within 6 months and conversion up at least a point.' If a cheap test can settle it, I'd run it. If not, I'd write a one-page decision document with the options, my recommendation and the evidence that would change it, and take it to the person who owns the decision above both of them, having shown each stakeholder the document first so neither is surprised. I'd tell them both what was decided and why, and credit what each contributed. I'd avoid picking the more senior person by default, and I'd avoid a compromise that serves neither goal.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Deep understanding of both sides; criteria before the choice; options; no surprises; fairness.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the common manager won't decide? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask what is making it hard (missing information, a political cost) and address that; and if there is no decision, I'd propose a time-boxed trial with the criteria agreed, so that the decision is made by the data after two weeks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if one stakeholder goes around you to the executive? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd speak to them directly and without accusation: 'I heard you took it to [the executive]; I want to make sure you have the full picture; here is the document I'm preparing.' And I'd make sure the executive has the same one-page view.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-07 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You must decide with about 60% of the data you'd like. How do you communicate your recommendation?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd say I'm not sure and ask for more time.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the recommendation with a confidence level.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the uncertainty statement: what we know, assume, confidence as a number, reversibility, what would change the decision and when it will be checked; time-boxes further data; recommends anyway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd give a clear recommendation, say how sure I am, and say how we'll find out if I'm wrong. For example: 'Based on a two-week test of 8,000 users, conversion rose 2.1% (interval 0.6% to 3.6%). That is the 60% of the data I'd like: we haven't seen a full month or the mobile app. The interval is above zero, but it leaves out novelty and the mobile app, so I'm about 80% confident the effect holds, and I'd expect it to be between 1% and 3%. The change is reversible within a day. So I recommend shipping to 25% of users now and checking again Friday, with a rule: if conversion falls by more than 1%, we roll back. Waiting for the full month costs us about $20,000 in forgone conversions and would reduce my uncertainty only a little, because the interval is already narrowing.'</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd say what I assume (that the second half of the month looks like the first) and what would change my mind (a drop on mobile). I'd put a date on the next look. And I would not hide behind the uncertainty: after stating it, I recommend.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A recommendation with calibrated confidence; reversibility; a trigger and a check date; the cost of waiting.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How did you get to &quot;80% confident&quot;? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;From the interval: most of it lies above zero, so with a simple model there's a high chance the true effect is positive; I round down because of what we haven't seen. I'd say 'about 80%' to signal it's a judgment, and I'd be happy to hear if someone sees it differently.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the stakes were irreversible? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I'd want more data, and I'd say what the cost of waiting is, and I'd set a deadline for the decision so that waiting doesn't become a decision by default. I'd also look for a reversible way to learn more first, such as a limited pilot.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-08 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You work with a team about twelve hours away in time. How do you run a project?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd schedule meetings at a time that works for both.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses asynchronous writing, handoffs and limited overlap hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Writes for asynchronous reading, protects overlap for discussion and decisions, hands off with a written note, shares the inconvenience of timing, makes decisions and ownership explicit, and checks understanding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd assume we can't rely on live time, and design for that. Everything that needs a decision or has context goes in a document with the answer, the ask and the deadline in the first paragraph, so that the other side can act while I'm asleep. For overlap, say two hours a day, I'd keep it for what needs live discussion: disagreements, complex tradeoffs and relationship. Everything else goes into writing.</p>\n<p>&quot;At the end of my day I'd write a handoff: what's done, what's blocked, what's next and what I need by when. They'd do the same for me. For meetings I'd rotate the awkward time so that one side isn't always stretching, with an agenda and notes with decisions and owners afterward. I'd make ownership explicit, since silence at a distance is easy to misread, and I'd check understanding by asking the other side to restate the decision. And I'd invest in the relationship early, with a call to get to know each other, since trust makes asynchronous work easier.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Async-first writing; protected overlap; handoffs; fairness; explicit ownership.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A decision is blocking and the other side is asleep. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;If it is reversible and within my authority, decide and document why, and tell them first thing; if not, I'd ask the owner for a delegate who can decide in my hours, or I'd prepare the decision document so that they can approve within minutes when they wake.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep from misreading tone in chat? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'm explicit and a little warmer in writing than feels natural, I say what I want, and I move anything with an edge to a call.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-09 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you make sure a message is understood by engineers, designers and legal?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I send the same message to everyone.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tailors the message to each audience's concerns.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Starts from shared facts, gives each audience the version that answers their question, avoids jargon, checks understanding by asking for a restatement, and keeps one source of truth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I keep one source of truth, a short document with the facts, the decision and the ask, and give each group the framing that answers their question. Engineers want the scope, the constraint and the date: what exactly are we building, what is out, what are the risks? Designers want the user problem, the evidence and the freedom to solve it: what are we trying to change for whom? Legal wants the exposure, the data involved and the markets, and wants it early. I avoid jargon that one group knows and another doesn't, and I define terms once.</p>\n<p>&quot;I check that the message landed by asking for a restatement ('Can you tell me in your words what we're doing and what you need from me?') or by asking someone outside the team to read the document and say the point. If two groups hear different things, I bring them together with the same one-pager. And I send decisions in writing after a meeting so that memory is not the record.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> One truth, several framings; audience's concerns; checking understanding.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if legal's concerns arrive after the design is done? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd treat it as a lesson about process: bring legal in at the problem stage in the future, not the end. For now I'd ask what is the smallest change that meets the requirement, and present options to design with the real constraint.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you explain a technical constraint to a designer without condescension? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I ask the engineer to explain it in terms of what the user would experience, not how the system works, and I bring examples. I frame it as a design constraint to work within, not as a no.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-10 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You disagree with a data scientist's interpretation of an experiment. How do you resolve it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd trust the data scientist,&quot; or &quot;I'd argue my view.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the method and looks at the data together.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Restates their interpretation, agrees the question and the metric, examines assumptions (sample, duration, novelty, segments), proposes a check that could settle it, and decides who decides and how to proceed in the meantime.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd start by making sure I understand their reading and what it rests on: 'Walk me through how you got to this. What does the interval say, and what would make you change your mind?' I often learn something. Then I'd say my concern concretely: 'I'm wondering whether the lift is novelty, since the first week was +4% and the last was +1%.' We'd agree what question the test answers and which metric is primary, because many disagreements are about different questions.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then I'd look for what would settle it: reading the effect by week, checking segments, extending the test or running a holdout. We'd agree in advance what each result would mean for the decision. If we still disagree, we'd write both readings with the uncertainty and take the decision to the owner, with a recommendation about the next step that is reversible. I'd credit the data scientist's contribution, and if the data shows I was wrong, I'd say so. My aim is the right decision and a working relationship, since I'll need their judgment again.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Understanding the method; a specific concern; agreed criteria; humility; a path to a decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the data scientist is much more senior? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Seniority doesn't change the data. I'd bring the specific concern with evidence and ask for their view on it; senior people usually welcome a precise challenge. If they hold their view, I'd ask what check would convince them, and we'd run it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you realize you misunderstood the analysis? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I say so promptly and thank them. It's cheaper to be wrong in a conversation than in a launch, and it makes the next disagreement easier.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-11\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-11 · Hard · behavioral · BE · Stripe</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Write the message to executives that a key launch will slip by three weeks because another team's dependency is late.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A long message that apologizes, blames the other team and leaves the ask unclear.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the new date, the cause, the impact and the plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Facts without blame, a calibrated date with a range, options and a recommendation, what is being done, what is needed and by when, and an offer to discuss; written so that a reader can forward it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I describe a writing style and make no claim about any real company's practice.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Subject: Launch of the new payouts flow moves from 12 November to 3 December; decision needed by Thursday</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Summary.</strong> The launch will slip three weeks, to 3 December. I'm about 70% confident of that date and would plan on 10 December as the date we can stand behind.</p>\n<p><strong>Cause.</strong> The identity-verification service we depend on will be ready on 24 November, not 3 November. The date was set by the team that owns it; we found out on 28 October when their testing found a defect.</p>\n<p><strong>Impact.</strong> We miss the start of the holiday campaign; about 15% of planned volume (about $400,000) moves into December.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> (A) Ship everything on 3 December. (B) Ship on 12 November without identity verification for returning users (about 80% of volume), adding new users on 3 December. Risk: a split campaign message and a lower verification rate in the interim, which we would cap with limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> B, if marketing confirms it can run a partial campaign.</p>\n<p><strong>What I need.</strong> Your decision on A or B by Thursday, so marketing and engineering can plan.</p>\n<p><strong>What we're doing.</strong> Daily check-ins with the owning team; we've agreed milestones on 12 and 19 November; I'll update you every Tuesday and immediately if the date moves.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><em>Why it works.</em> The subject line has the news and the ask. It states facts about the cause without blame (&quot;the date was set by the team that owns it&quot;), gives a range, and offers options with a recommendation and a deadline. It can be forwarded without edits.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Do you name the other team? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Yes, factually, since leaders need to know whom to talk to, and without judgment. I'd have already told that team's lead what I'm sending, so it is not a surprise, and I'd ask them to check the facts.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the other team contests your account? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask what's different and correct anything that is wrong right away. If we disagree about a judgment, I'd write both views in a neutral line and offer a call with the three of us. My aim is for leaders to have the same picture we have.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c32-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented, and all scenarios are Illustrative; use only what is true in your own work.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-01 · Rebuild an answer, answer first</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite this into an answer-first version of 60 to 90 words: &quot;So we started by looking at the data and we noticed a few things, and then we had a couple of meetings with the team, and after a lot of back and forth we decided to cut the number of fields in the sign-up form, and then we tested it, and I think it did better, something like six points.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Practice with a partner.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent two supporting facts and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-01 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;Cutting the sign-up form from 12 fields to 7 raised completion by 6 points, from 52% to 58% (invented). Two things drove it. First, the funnel showed 38% of people stopped at the address step, so we removed the fields that could wait until after sign-up. Second, we added inline validation so that errors appeared as people typed. The test ran for three weeks on a 50% split and the lift held each week. Next I'd like to test the same idea on the payment step.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Lead with the next step if the interviewer asks about plans.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Answer-first can sound abrupt; a warm, clear first sentence solves that.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-02 · Rewrite a status update</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite as a four-line update (FW-C32-03): &quot;This week was busy! I had lots of meetings with the team and talked to a few customers. We're still working on the dashboard and I think it'll be done soon. There were some issues with the data that I'm looking into. I also reviewed the designs. Next week I'll keep working on all of this. Let me know if you need anything.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A weekly update.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent facts and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;<strong>At risk: dashboard launch may slip two days because of a data issue; one decision needed.</strong> <strong>Changed:</strong> Customer calls (5) showed that the weekly view is the one people want, so we will ship that first; a data issue (duplicate events for 3% of users) is being fixed by engineering. <strong>Next:</strong> fix verified Tuesday; designs approved Wednesday. <strong>Need from you:</strong> Decide by Tuesday whether to launch the weekly view without the monthly view.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If the data issue is minor, change the headline to &quot;On track&quot; and move the issue to &quot;Changed.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A candid headline invites questions; it also lets people help.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-03 · Write a decision document</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a one-page decision document (FW-C32-02) for: &quot;Ship the new onboarding on Monday with a known minor bug (affects 1% of users, cosmetic), or delay a week.&quot; Invent supporting numbers and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A real decision memo.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ten minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-02 and Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;<strong>Decision needed:</strong> Ship Monday with a cosmetic bug or delay a week; needed by Friday, from the head of product. <strong>Context:</strong> The new onboarding raised completion from 41% to 47% in a test; a cosmetic misalignment affects 1% of users (about 600 a month); a one-week delay would cost about 1,200 additional completions at the 6-point lift (invented). <strong>Options:</strong> A: ship Monday and fix in the next release (cost: 600 users a month see a misaligned button; risk: low). B: delay a week (cost: about 1,200 fewer completions; risk: none from the bug). C: ship Monday to 50% and fix within three days. <strong>Recommendation:</strong> C, which captures about half the gain immediately and exposes about 300 users a month to a cosmetic issue for three days. <strong>What would change my mind:</strong> if support reports confusion, or if the bug affects more than 3% of users. <strong>Risks and next steps:</strong> engineering fixes by Wednesday; support has a note; I review daily.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Recommend A with a support note if the bug is truly cosmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A staged release reduces exposure and adds coordination.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-04 · Map the stakeholders</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You propose raising the price of a plan by 10%. Fill in a stakeholder map (FW-C32-05) for six stakeholders: finance, sales, support, engineering, marketing and the head of product (decider), with power, interest, concern, likely position and your plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A contested change.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-05 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Finance:</em> high power, high interest; wants revenue and forecast accuracy; champion; give the model and the range. <em>Sales:</em> medium power, high interest; fears harder deals and discount requests; skeptic; ask what discount data shows and agree guardrails. <em>Support:</em> low power, high interest; fears angry customers; neutral; prepare a script and a volume estimate. <em>Engineering:</em> medium power, low interest; wants the change to be a configuration, not code; neutral; confirm it is simple. <em>Marketing:</em> medium power, medium interest; messaging and timing; neutral; co-write the message. <em>head of product:</em> high power, medium interest; wants a defensible decision; pre-wire with the decision document and the test plan (price test on new customers first).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add customers as a stakeholder group and plan a communication.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A thorough map costs an afternoon and avoids a late blocker.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-05 · Sequence the pre-wiring</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You need engineering, legal and a skeptical growth lead to support a new consent step. In what order do you talk to them, what do you ask each, and what do you say to the skeptic?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Influence without authority.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Legal first:</em> confirm the requirement in writing, so that the proposal is not just a preference. <em>Analyst:</em> get a range of the impact. <em>Growth lead second:</em> the skeptic should not hear it from someone else; acknowledge the loss, show the range, propose a test with a guardrail, and ask what result would persuade them. <em>Engineering third:</em> scope, risk and a rollback flag. Then design, then the decider. To the skeptic: &quot;I agree the loss matters; that's why I want to test two designs. If the lighter one costs under half a point we ship it; if not, we change the design. Counsel has confirmed doing nothing isn't an option here.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Talk to the engineer first if the effort estimate is the likeliest blocker.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The order is a judgment: start with whoever's input changes the proposal most.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-06 · Write three no scripts</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write short no scripts (FW-C32-07) for: (a) a sales director asking for a custom report in two days; (b) your manager asking for a feature in addition to a full plan; (c) a stakeholder asking you to hide a known bug from a customer.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Saying no.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;I see the goal: the customer needs numbers for their review. I can't build the report in two days; it's about a week. I can send you a spreadsheet extract today that covers most of it, and I'll build the report if two more customers ask. Does the extract work for their review?&quot; (b) &quot;We can add it, and to hold the date something leaves. Options: drop the export, or move the launch by two weeks. Which do you prefer?&quot; (c) &quot;I can't do that: hiding a known bug from a customer risks their trust and our obligations. What I can do is tell them about it with the workaround and the fix date; I'll draft the message now, and we can send it together.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (a), offer a time-boxed manual version with a clear end date.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A firm no on (c) may cost goodwill; the cost of hiding it is higher.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-07 · State the uncertainty</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A test on 6,000 users shows a 1.8% lift in conversion with a 95% interval of −0.2% to +3.8%. The change can be rolled back in a day. Write the uncertainty statement (FW-C32-08) and a recommendation.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Incomplete data.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-08 and IQ-C32-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The interval is about ±2 points wide, so the standard error is about 1.0 point, and the estimate is about 1.8 standard errors above zero; under a simple normal approximation the chance the true lift is positive is about 96%. &quot;Based on a test of 6,000 users, conversion rose 1.8% (interval −0.2% to +3.8%). I'm about 90% confident the effect is positive; I round down because we have only two weeks of data and haven't seen the mobile app separately. I expect a true effect between 0% and 3%. The change is reversible within a day, so I recommend shipping to 25% of users and checking on Friday; if conversion falls by more than 1%, we roll back. What would change my mind: a negative reading on mobile.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Extend the test a week if the cost of waiting is small and the change is not easily reversible.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A confident statement invites challenge; that is the point.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-08 · Meeting notes and follow-up</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> From these raw notes, write a follow-up message (decisions, owners, dates, open questions): &quot;Talked about launch. Priya says the bug is minor. Sam thinks we should wait. Leo said legal needs a day. We'll probably go Monday unless something comes up. Someone should tell marketing. Open: whether to turn off export.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> After a meeting.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C32.9.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;Thanks all. <strong>Decisions:</strong> Launch Monday, subject to legal sign-off by Friday noon (Leo). <strong>Owners:</strong> Leo, legal review, Friday noon. Priya, confirm the bug is cosmetic by Thursday and share the repro steps. I will tell marketing today. <strong>Open question:</strong> whether to turn off export for launch; Sam and Priya to bring a recommendation by Thursday; I decide Friday. <strong>Risk raised:</strong> Sam's concern that a bug could affect first impressions; mitigation: staged rollout to 25%. Please reply with corrections by tomorrow.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a one-line summary at the top if the thread is long.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Precision on owners and dates takes a minute and prevents the &quot;I thought you were doing it&quot; problem.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-09 · Time-zone arithmetic and handoff</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You work from India (IST, UTC+5:30) with a nine-to-six day. Compute the overlap with a London team (nine to six, GMT in winter, UTC+1 in summer) and with a California team (nine to six Pacific, UTC−8 in winter). Propose a plan for the California team.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Remote work.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C32.10.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Your day is 03:30 to 12:30 UTC. London winter (GMT): 09:00 to 18:00 UTC, overlap 09:00 to 12:30: 3.5 hours; London summer (BST, UTC+1): 08:00 to 17:00 UTC, overlap 08:00 to 12:30: 4.5 hours. California winter (UTC−8): 17:00 to 02:00 UTC, no overlap with your day; California 06:30 is 14:30 UTC, which is 20:00 in India. So a live meeting needs the Californian to start at 06:30 to 08:30 and you to work 20:00 to 22:00. Plan: write for asynchronous reading with the ask and deadline first; one live call a week, alternating inconvenience (one week your evening, the next their early morning); a daily written handoff at the end of each day; a decision log; delegates who can decide in each region.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Shift your own day one or two days a week (for example to 12:00 to 21:00) so that the overlap is shorter and less lopsided.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fairness in awkward hours matters for morale and for the quality of meetings.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-10 · A timed executive update with interruptions</h5>\n";
